From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: Select on-board WiFi antenna on Radxa ZERO 3W

The board routes the WiFi antenna through an RF switch controlled by
GPIO3_D3 (on-board PCB antenna) and GPIO3_D2 (external U.FL connector).
Radxa's vendor device tree drives GPIO3_D3 low at boot; mainline leaves
both pins unconfigured, so the switch floats and the radio ends up on the
normally unpopulated U.FL socket.  The AIC8800 then scans every channel
and completes the scan normally, but never receives a beacon, which looks
like a broken driver rather than a dead antenna path.

Hog GPIO3_D3 low so the on-board antenna is selected, matching the vendor
default.  Verified on a Radxa ZERO 3W: with the pin driven low a scan
immediately returns the surrounding networks.
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w.dts	2026-11-13 21:18:11.768093944 +0200
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w.dts	2026-11-13 21:18:22.259880169 +0200
@@ -26,6 +26,24 @@
 	};
 };
 
+&gpio3 {
+	/*
+	 * The WiFi antenna is routed through an RF switch.  Radxa's vendor
+	 * device tree drives GPIO3_D3 low to select the on-board PCB antenna,
+	 * while their "external antenna" overlay drives GPIO3_D2 instead to
+	 * select the U.FL connector.  Mainline configures neither pin, so the
+	 * switch is left floating on the usually unpopulated U.FL socket: the
+	 * AIC8800 then scans every channel and completes normally, but never
+	 * receives a single beacon.
+	 */
+	wifi_antenna_hog: wifi-antenna-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <RK_PD3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		output-low;
+		line-name = "wifi-antenna-onboard";
+	};
+};
+
 &pinctrl {
 	bluetooth {
 		bt_reg_on_h: bt-reg-on-h {
